<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Pius II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1405">1405</a>–<a href="/years/1464">1464</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Enea Silvio Piccolomini, Aeneas Sylvius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pius II, born Enea Silvio Piccolomini, was a humanist scholar and pope from 1458 to 1464. He tried to organise a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> from the last despot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> and condemned Sigismondo Malatesta of Rimini. His Commentaries are a unique papal autobiography.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Pius II" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pinturicchio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pintoricchio_014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Patrons made the movement possible: the Medici in Florence, Federico da Montefeltro at Urbino, Alfonso V at Naples, Pope Nicholas V, who commissioned Latin translations of the Greek historians and gathered the books from which the Vatican Library grew, and <b>Pope Pius II</b>, himself a humanist writer.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Pius II</b> publicly condemned Sigismondo in 1462 and took most of his lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-rimini">Lordship of Rimini</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1460 <b>Pope Pius II</b> converted the abbey into a secular collegiate foundation headed by a prince-provost.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-provostry-of-ellwangen">Prince-Provostry of Ellwangen</a></li><li><blockquote>When Mehmed II conquered the Morea in 1460, the despot Thomas Palaiologos handed it to <b>Pope Pius II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-monemvasia">Venetian Monemvasia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pienza, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Ancona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.08,11.68&amp;year=1443" aria-label="Pienza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4832 634 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Pius II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5282 904h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5282 904h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.08,11.68&amp;year=1443">What was here in 1443? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
